+# Decision: Self-Hosted WebSockets for Committed-Change Delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard serves up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers. Editors make relatively few changes compared with the number of connected viewers: a busy market averages two status changes per minute, with bursts around opening. The board needs to show committed changes within 15 seconds under normal connectivity and restore the latest board after a browser has been disconnected for up to 20 minutes.
+
+The board has one authoritative revision sequence per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; this decision concerns how committed revisions reach browsers. Delivery, revision storage, and browser recovery must work together so that a missed live update can be recovered without treating a disconnection as an unchanged board.
+
+Three approaches meet the broad freshness goal: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, and a hosted realtime service. A subsecond delivery guarantee is not required. The decision weighs responsiveness and vendor independence against the operational work of running live connections.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Fast updates — major.** Reduce the wait between a committed change and its display in a connected browser. The 15-second project goal is an upper bound under normal connectivity; faster updates are useful, but this decision does not establish a subsecond service-level target.
+- **Avoid realtime-provider fees and coupling — major.** Do not make board delivery dependent on a realtime vendor's service contract, client model, or continued availability. Self-hosting still has infrastructure and engineering costs; this criterion concerns the realtime-service dependency.
+- **Operational burden — moderate.** Limit the work required to operate delivery for up to 800 simultaneous browsers, including connection capacity, publication reliability, and recovery after disconnects. A hosted service can reduce connection operations, while polling avoids maintaining live sessions.
+- **Repeated request and read load — moderate.** Avoid work proportional to the number of browsers polling when committed changes are infrequent. The project supports hundreds of simultaneous viewers and modest steady edit volume, with short bursts around opening.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Browsers issue an HTTPS request every five seconds, asking for revisions after their last applied event revision. The server responds with changes since that cursor. Delivery remains request/response; the browser owns the polling loop. The store must support cursor queries, and a browser that was away can catch up by requesting later revisions.
+
+- **Responsiveness — partial.** An update may wait for the next poll, adding up to roughly one polling interval before request and processing time.
+- **Provider independence — strong.** The design uses the application's HTTPS service and does not depend on a realtime vendor.
+- **Operational burden — strong.** The server does not maintain a session for every connected browser, and reconnecting resumes ordinary HTTPS requests.
+- **Repeated request and read load — weak.** Requests continue for every polling browser even when no event revision has changed.
+
+Summary: simpler stateless delivery, traded for repeated reads and polling-interval lag.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The application maintains WebSocket sessions with connected browsers and pushes committed changes as they are published. The change store must retain replayable revisions and publish committed changes reliably. Delivery owns session lifecycle and connection capacity. Browsers track the last applied revision, reconnect after interruptions, and catch up from that revision so a missed push does not lose a change.
+
+- **Responsiveness — strong.** Connected clients can receive a change when it is published rather than waiting for their next poll.
+- **Provider independence — strong.** The project operates the delivery path without a hosted realtime-service dependency.
+- **Operational burden — weak.** The project owns connection capacity, session lifecycle, reliable publication to active sessions, and reconnect behavior under failures or service restarts.
+- **Repeated request and read load — strong.** Connected browsers do not repeatedly request the board while it is unchanged; replay queries are used for initial state and catch-up.
+
+Summary: fast push delivery without realtime-vendor coupling, traded for connection and recovery operations.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+The application publishes committed revisions to a hosted realtime provider, which maintains browser subscriptions and delivers updates. The application still needs to reconcile publication with committed storage so that a successful board edit is not silently lost between persistence and publication. Delivery must integrate provider credentials and publication behavior; browsers use the provider's subscription and recovery model.
+
+- **Responsiveness — strong.** The provider can push changes to subscribed browsers without a polling interval.
+- **Provider independence — weak.** Delivery depends on the provider's service, integration, client model, and pricing.
+- **Operational burden — strong.** The provider operates the browser connection fleet, although the application still owns publication reconciliation and provider integration.
+- **Repeated request and read load — strong.** Browsers receive pushed changes rather than querying for every interval with no change.
+
+Summary: low connection-operation burden and fast delivery, traded for recurring fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+Choose self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery. This gives connected browsers updates without waiting for a polling cycle and avoids a realtime-service vendor dependency. It also avoids repeated idle polling reads proportional to the number of browsers when no revisions have changed.
+
+The choice accepts the operational cost that polling and a hosted service reduce: the project must provision and operate connection capacity, publish committed revisions reliably to active sessions, and make browser reconnect and catch-up correct. Those responsibilities are part of the design, not optional resilience enhancements. If live publication is interrupted, browsers must be able to resume from their last applied revision and recover the current board from retained event history.
+
+The choice does not promise subsecond delivery or eliminate infrastructure cost. It establishes push delivery as the normal path and revision-based recovery as the path after missed updates. The change-store node must define replayable revisions and reliable publication; the delivery node must define WebSocket session lifecycle and capacity behavior; the browser-sync node must define subscription, reconnect, and catch-up behavior. The existing 15-second normal-connectivity goal, 20-minute recovery window, and seven-day revision retention remain the project-level targets.
